00:49:52.170 --> 00:49:54.910
was when you went on tour with your dad, right?

1050
00:49:54.910 --> 00:49:57.100
So he gets picked up by Paul Simon

1051
00:49:57.100 --> 00:49:58.830
to go on the Graceland tour.

1052
00:49:58.830 --> 00:50:01.230
Yeah.
And you're in Sydney, right?

1053
00:50:01.230 --> 00:50:02.063
Yes.

1054
00:50:03.040 --> 00:50:06.510
I hadn't seen my dad for about four and a half, five years

1055
00:50:06.510 --> 00:50:09.930
because he had decided to go and make music in Africa.

1056
00:50:09.930 --> 00:50:12.110
Like he couldn't go to South Africa,

1057
00:50:12.110 --> 00:50:14.000
but he just had to be on the continent.

1058
00:50:14.000 --> 00:50:15.370
So after my fifth grade graduation,

1059
00:50:15.370 --> 00:50:18.060
he just like pulled up and was gone.

1060
00:50:18.060 --> 00:50:19.980
I got a saxophone for my birthday

1061
00:50:19.980 --> 00:50:22.960
and then I didn't see my dad for like four and a half years

1062
00:50:22.960 --> 00:50:24.440
which was a trip, like we'd write letters,

1063
00:50:24.440 --> 00:50:25.570
talk on the phone once in a while.

1064
00:50:25.570 --> 00:50:27.370
But he was on a quest.

1065
00:50:27.370 --> 00:50:28.460
It was a wall.

1066
00:50:28.460 --> 00:50:30.930
Trying to get as close as possible

1067
00:50:30.930 --> 00:50:33.160
to the thing as possible.

1068
00:50:33.160 --> 00:50:34.560
So real artist.

1069
00:50:34.560 --> 00:50:35.980
Yeah.

1070
00:50:35.980 --> 00:50:37.830
But there's repercussions to that.

1071
00:50:37.830 --> 00:50:39.630
Yeah, you also don't understand like what do you,

1072
00:50:39.630 --> 00:50:41.310
what, where are you, man?

1073
00:50:41.310 --> 00:50:43.150
Like I'm dying over here.

1074
00:50:43.150 --> 00:50:45.530
Like, you don't understand what they put me through.

1075
00:50:45.530 --> 00:50:48.160
And we went to go see him.

1076
00:50:48.160 --> 00:50:51.140
He reached out and said he was on tour with Paul Simon.

1077
00:50:51.140 --> 00:50:54.970
At this point, like call me out is like on M.T.V every day,

1078
00:50:54.970 --> 00:50:56.730
like that album's blowing up.

1079
00:50:56.730 --> 00:50:58.800
It was huge.
Huge.

1080
00:50:58.800 --> 00:51:01.310
How did that, did he, was he on the album too?

1081
00:51:01.310 --> 00:51:03.060
So he wasn't on the album

1082
00:51:03.060 --> 00:51:05.520
because Paul went to South Africa to make the album

1083
00:51:05.520 --> 00:51:07.730
but my dad made it possible

1084
00:51:07.730 --> 00:51:10.530
for Paul to get into South Africa

1085
00:51:10.530 --> 00:51:13.723
and coordinated the musicians for him to work with.

1086
00:51:14.580 --> 00:51:17.110
Like, and Paul was making that album in secret

1087
00:51:17.110 --> 00:51:18.890
in South Africa, which is crazy.

1088
00:51:18.890 --> 00:51:21.870
Also like Paul Simon was a gangster

1089
00:51:21.870 --> 00:51:24.640
like a real, a real g.

1090
00:51:24.640 --> 00:51:27.640
I'm trying to remember what the state of apartheid

1091
00:51:27.640 --> 00:51:28.750
was at that time.

1092
00:51:28.750 --> 00:51:30.320
Peak.
Yeah.

1093
00:51:30.320 --> 00:51:31.230
Peak like bad.

1094
00:51:31.230 --> 00:51:34.060
So he had to do it completely undercover.

1095
00:51:34.060 --> 00:51:35.800
On the low.
Wow.

1096
00:51:35.800 --> 00:51:39.620
And in exchange, you know,

1097
00:51:39.620 --> 00:51:42.900
to acknowledge what my dad did to help facilitate that,

1098
00:51:42.900 --> 00:51:45.520
he not only took my father on tour

1099
00:51:45.520 --> 00:51:47.380
but he made him a headliner.

1100
00:51:47.380 --> 00:51:50.460
He shared, so it was the Graceland tour

1101
00:51:50.460 --> 00:51:52.563
and then the headliners were Paul Simon,

1102
00:51:53.470 --> 00:51:56.770
Ladysmith Black Mambazo, Miriam Makeba, Hugh Masekela.

1103
00:51:56.770 --> 00:51:57.781
Wow.

1104
00:51:57.781 --> 00:52:02.781
That is how Paul, that's how every poster was.

1105
00:52:03.020 --> 00:52:04.570
That's what the billing was

1106
00:52:04.570 --> 00:52:09.000
and the entirety of the show was him storytelling

1107
00:52:09.000 --> 00:52:10.470
how important this music was

1108
00:52:10.470 --> 00:52:12.950
and then giving them the platform and the stage

1109
00:52:12.950 --> 00:52:15.270
to these African artists to shine

1110
00:52:15.270 --> 00:52:18.960
like super, super, super progressive.

1111
00:52:18.960 --> 00:52:20.810
And he was taking a lot of shit for it.

1112
00:52:20.810 --> 00:52:21.643
You know, there were people

1113
00:52:21.643 --> 00:52:23.500
who thought he was being exploitative.

1114
00:52:25.370 --> 00:52:26.930
I remember being in the press conferences

1115
00:52:26.930 --> 00:52:29.680
where, you know, they would try to jump on my father

1116
00:52:29.680 --> 00:52:30.513
and be like, you know,

1117
00:52:30.513 --> 00:52:33.600
isn't he a culture vulture, et cetera.

1118
00:52:33.600 --> 00:52:36.370
My dad would be like, what are you talking about, man?

1119
00:52:36.370 --> 00:52:38.410
Like, do you not see what we're doing here?

1120
00:52:38.410 --> 00:52:41.740
We're sitting here in this international press conference

1121
00:52:41.740 --> 00:52:44.290
and we're talking about South Africa and like,

1122
00:52:44.290 --> 00:52:47.620
this is gospel to people to be concerned, to be curious,

1123
00:52:47.620 --> 00:52:48.680
to wanna know why

1124
00:52:48.680 --> 00:52:51.440
and to start demanding of their governments,

1125
00:52:51.440 --> 00:52:53.870
to not like wanna participate in this thing.

1126
00:52:53.870 --> 00:52:57.640
And it really, I do believe that that was a huge part

1127
00:52:57.640 --> 00:53:00.560
in shifting the world's consciousness about apartheid.

1128
00:53:00.560 --> 00:53:02.640
100%, it is complicated though,

1129
00:53:02.640 --> 00:53:05.890
because the argument is obvious that,

1130
00:53:05.890 --> 00:53:07.640
look here's a white guy, you know,

1131
00:53:07.640 --> 00:53:09.630
so he's gonna be the one who's gonna introduce

1132
00:53:09.630 --> 00:53:11.750
all these forms of music to the world.

1133
00:53:11.750 --> 00:53:14.810
But in truth, that's what he did and he exposed,

1134
00:53:14.810 --> 00:53:18.730
he gave a level of exposure to a certain type of music

1135
00:53:18.730 --> 00:53:22.770
and provided a cultural lens on, you know,

1136
00:53:22.770 --> 00:53:25.170
civil rights and social dilemma that was going on

1137
00:53:25.170 --> 00:53:28.480
in a manner that could not have been achieved otherwise.

1138
00:53:28.480 --> 00:53:29.700
But he's still a white dude

1139
00:53:29.700 --> 00:53:31.540
kind of taken a bunch of black people.

1140
00:53:31.540 --> 00:53:34.030
Yeah, and he was gonna take, that he was down.

1141
00:53:34.030 --> 00:53:35.800
But they were playing the music.

1142
00:53:35.800 --> 00:53:36.769
They were playing the music.
You know,

1143
00:53:36.769 --> 00:53:38.120
that's the difference between, you know,

1144
00:53:38.120 --> 00:53:40.243
Elvis or the Stones or someone like that.

1145
00:53:40.243 --> 00:53:42.620
I mean, they were all playing the music

1146
00:53:42.620 --> 00:53:44.900
and my dad had his back.

1147
00:53:44.900 --> 00:53:49.120
It was like their relationship was very special.

1148
00:53:49.120 --> 00:53:52.660
But anyway, we went to go see that show in New York,

1149
00:53:52.660 --> 00:53:55.030
my mother and I and just spend a couple of days with my dad.

1150
00:53:55.030 --> 00:53:58.380
And Paul Simon was the one who just said

1151
00:53:58.380 --> 00:53:59.670
you should come on the road with us.

1152
00:53:59.670 --> 00:54:02.053
And I looked at him and I laughed.

1153
00:54:02.053 --> 00:54:03.100
I was like, what?

1154
00:54:03.100 --> 00:54:04.650
This is no way my parents like,

1155
00:54:04.650 --> 00:54:06.370
he's like, well I think you need to spend some time

1156
00:54:06.370 --> 00:54:08.720
with your dad I'm gonna talk to your mother.

1157
00:54:08.720 --> 00:54:10.680
And I remember backstage it was like,

1158
00:54:10.680 --> 00:54:12.570
I mean, the third night I see Paul

1159
00:54:12.570 --> 00:54:15.920
like he talked to me about what he was gonna do.

1160
00:54:15.920 --> 00:54:19.080
My dad had told Paul, like there's no way

1161
00:54:19.080 --> 00:54:21.617
his mother won't allow, don't even ask.

1162
00:54:21.617 --> 00:54:24.760
And I see Paul in the corner, man doing this thing like

1163
00:54:24.760 --> 00:54:26.833
just G and my mother loved him.

1164
00:54:28.020 --> 00:54:29.130
She's not gonna say no to him.

1165
00:54:29.130 --> 00:54:32.910
Yeah, she was like, I saw her nodding

1166
00:54:32.910 --> 00:54:35.830
and we went back home to New England.

1167
00:54:35.830 --> 00:54:38.500
I remember that fight.

1168
00:54:38.500 --> 00:54:43.500
I remember the debate between my mother and stepfather.

1169
00:54:43.630 --> 00:54:47.340
I was in my bedroom, like listening with my ear to the door.

1170
00:54:47.340 --> 00:54:52.120
I could hear the screaming and I knew my mom had won

1171
00:54:52.120 --> 00:54:53.700
like there was a silence.

1172
00:54:53.700 --> 00:54:55.210
I didn't hear my stepfather speak anymore.

1173
00:54:55.210 --> 00:54:57.740
I was like, this shit might be happening.

1174
00:54:57.740 --> 00:54:59.497
And he came by, I was afraid he walked by me

1175
00:54:59.497 --> 00:55:03.430
and he said, well, I guess this is what you want

1176
00:55:03.430 --> 00:55:05.670
so good luck.

1177
00:55:05.670 --> 00:55:07.193
I remember when he drove me to the trains.

1178
00:55:07.193 --> 00:55:08.930
Like this air of disappointment.

1179
00:55:08.930 --> 00:55:09.960
Total disappointment.

1180
00:55:09.960 --> 00:55:12.072
When he drove me to this train station

1181
00:55:12.072 --> 00:55:14.840
to go take the plane to New York,

1182
00:55:14.840 --> 00:55:17.420
to go get the Amtrak to New York.

1183
00:55:17.420 --> 00:55:19.680
It was one of those scenes where it wasn't like

1184
00:55:19.680 --> 00:55:21.960
it was silent, it was like, bye.

1185
00:55:21.960 --> 00:55:24.816
You know, like he had taken the L with my mom

1186
00:55:24.816 --> 00:55:28.110
and I was just like yes.

1187
00:55:28.110 --> 00:55:30.300
What an insane life experience.

1188
00:55:30.300 --> 00:55:32.550
It was the greatest experience, Rich.

1189
00:55:32.550 --> 00:55:34.790
So you were like for three months right on the road?

1190
00:55:34.790 --> 00:55:36.830
Yeah, it was like almost famous.

1191
00:55:36.830 --> 00:55:41.150
The movie, like, it was kind of, there was a lot like that.

1192
00:55:41.150 --> 00:55:43.180
I saw a lot of things that I probably shouldn't have

1193
00:55:43.180 --> 00:55:48.180
at 15 and a half, but I also experienced so so much.

1194
00:55:49.170 --> 00:55:54.170
I mean, we're playing like 20, 30,000 person venues,

1195
00:55:54.980 --> 00:55:57.690
three, four nights in a row sold out.

1196
00:55:57.690 --> 00:56:00.260
Wow.
You know, watching my dad

1197
00:56:00.260 --> 00:56:04.610
just like destroy, you know, and dad and I are hanging out,

1198
00:56:04.610 --> 00:56:07.450
we're playing tennis every day or basketball with Paul.

1199
00:56:07.450 --> 00:56:10.810
And there was when we were in Sydney,

1200
00:56:10.810 --> 00:56:13.030
we were in Sydney for like a week and a half

1201
00:56:13.030 --> 00:56:16.310
and the local crew, you had your roadies

1202
00:56:16.310 --> 00:56:18.840
that were from the States and then we'd pick up local crew

1203
00:56:18.840 --> 00:56:20.120
in every city.

1204
00:56:20.120 --> 00:56:22.410
And one of the guys there had a surf board

1205
00:56:22.410 --> 00:56:27.120
and he'd always have it backstaged in the rigging.

1206
00:56:27.120 --> 00:56:28.850
I'd walked by it and I was fascinated by it

1207
00:56:28.850 --> 00:56:31.100
and I just like, bug him, like, hey, what's that?

1208
00:56:31.100 --> 00:56:33.110
And he told me about, I was like, I wanna see that,

1209
00:56:33.110 --> 00:56:34.580
like, can I see that?

1210
00:56:34.580 --> 00:56:35.600
Can you take me?

1211
00:56:35.600 --> 00:56:36.850
And he's like yeah man, yeah man,

1212
00:56:36.850 --> 00:56:37.890
I'll take you, I'll take you

1213
00:56:37.890 --> 00:56:40.090
and then finds like, I can't take him out,

1214
00:56:40.090 --> 00:56:44.580
I'm busy but you know, told me like where to get the ferry

1215
00:56:44.580 --> 00:56:46.180
and where I could see it.

1216
00:56:46.180 --> 00:56:51.180
So I'd set out one day, took a ferry to Bondai.

1217
00:56:51.240 --> 00:56:54.710
And, you know, he told me exactly where to go.

1218
00:56:54.710 --> 00:56:56.740
I bought a sandwich at this little sandwich shop

1219
00:56:56.740 --> 00:56:59.800
and I sat down on this bench and sure enough, that afternoon

1220
00:56:59.800 --> 00:57:02.930
two kids right around my age, come walking by.

1221
00:57:02.930 --> 00:57:04.060
They're holding these boards.

1222
00:57:04.060 --> 00:57:06.130
I'm like, all right, cool.

1223
00:57:06.130 --> 00:57:09.890
And they paddled out ways where maybe like two three feet

1224
00:57:09.890 --> 00:57:12.170
and they put on a show.

1225
00:57:12.170 --> 00:57:14.020
And the first thing to me, I was like

1226
00:57:14.020 --> 00:57:17.330
these kids are breakdancing on water.

1227
00:57:17.330 --> 00:57:20.800
This is dancing on water there's this freedom.

1228
00:57:20.800 --> 00:57:23.220
I could see the tech, like the expression.

1229
00:57:23.220 --> 00:57:24.490
I was a b-boy kid.

1230
00:57:24.490 --> 00:57:26.880
So I knew like, you know, there's this step flamboyancy

1231
00:57:26.880 --> 00:57:29.580
of like taking a move and making it your own

1232
00:57:29.580 --> 00:57:31.791
and I'm like, that's what they're doing on the surf board.

1233
00:57:31.791 --> 00:57:35.363
I'm like, I don't know where or how I'm going to do that.

1234
00:57:38.428 --> 00:57:39.960
It burnt into my brain.

1235
00:57:39.960 --> 00:57:42.770
And then a year later I come home from the tour,

1236
00:57:42.770 --> 00:57:45.100
a year later, I come home from school one day

1237
00:57:45.100 --> 00:57:46.880
and my stepfather says, sit down.

1238
00:57:46.880 --> 00:57:48.023
I'm like, what's up?

1239
00:57:48.023 --> 00:57:51.533
This is in April of my junior year high school.

1240
00:57:53.390 --> 00:57:54.940
We're moving to California in two weeks,

1241
00:57:54.940 --> 00:57:57.450
your mother doesn't wanna be cold anymore.

1242
00:57:57.450 --> 00:58:00.531
What are you talking about?

1243
00:58:00.531 --> 00:58:01.390
I have a girlfriend.

1244
00:58:01.390 --> 00:58:03.890
Like, I finally like figured out how to live here.

1245
00:58:03.890 --> 00:58:05.510
What do you mean?

1246
00:58:05.510 --> 00:58:07.510
I'm on my third high school as it is.

1247
00:58:07.510 --> 00:58:10.220
Right, and you're looking at your senior year.

1248
00:58:10.220 --> 00:58:12.151
If you transplant you're gonna start a new school

1249
00:58:12.151 --> 00:58:13.462
for your senior year.

1250
00:58:13.462 --> 00:58:14.470
For your senior year.
Yeah.

1251
00:58:14.470 --> 00:58:16.070
You're gonna finish out the last,

1252
00:58:16.070 --> 00:58:19.440
like five weeks in a new school of your junior year

1253
00:58:20.680 --> 00:58:22.030
in a new place.
Right.

1254
00:58:22.030 --> 00:58:23.640
And there was no point of reference

1255
00:58:23.640 --> 00:58:24.660
for what Carlsbad was.

1256
00:58:24.660 --> 00:58:27.390
I literally looked up in an encyclopedia.

1257
00:58:27.390 --> 00:58:29.810
I was like, got some, like some reference.

1258
00:58:29.810 --> 00:58:32.330
There was a famous dirt bike track at the time.

1259
00:58:32.330 --> 00:58:33.780
Why did they choose Carlsbad?

1260
00:58:33.780 --> 00:58:35.160
Was that a church thing too?

1261
00:58:35.160 --> 00:58:36.080
It was a hybrid.

1262
00:58:36.080 --> 00:58:41.030
I think they had made like a compromise

1263
00:58:41.030 --> 00:58:42.160
between the two of them.

1264
00:58:42.160 --> 00:58:44.570
My stepfather again, wanted to like

1265
00:58:45.930 --> 00:58:47.740
and the church is very organized

1266
00:58:47.740 --> 00:58:50.040
so you could literally like pick where you wanted to go

1267
00:58:50.040 --> 00:58:51.600
if you were at that point.

1268
00:58:51.600 --> 00:58:52.950
My stepfather was an elder.

1269
00:58:54.150 --> 00:58:55.710
Where do they need help?

1270
00:58:55.710 --> 00:58:57.810
And my stepfather's aspiration was that

1271
00:58:57.810 --> 00:58:59.840
we were gonna go move there

1272
00:58:59.840 --> 00:59:02.880
and then we were gonna go serve in a Spanish congregation.

1273
00:59:02.880 --> 00:59:04.210
'Cause my was Puerto Rican.

1274
00:59:04.210 --> 00:59:07.600
But didn't you get into some trouble in Australia

1275
00:59:07.600 --> 00:59:11.100
that caused a little consternation with the church?

1276
00:59:11.100 --> 00:59:15.550
I got into trouble in Australia, but I got into,

1277
00:59:15.550 --> 00:59:17.360
what caused consternation with the church

1278
00:59:17.360 --> 00:59:20.050
wasn't my trip to Australia.

1279
00:59:20.050 --> 00:59:22.980
I was young enough and I wasn't baptized yet

1280
00:59:22.980 --> 00:59:24.290
so I didn't really talk about the shit

1281
00:59:24.290 --> 00:59:26.130
that I'd got into in Australia.

1282
00:59:26.130 --> 00:59:31.080
It was a few years later when I was 19,

1283
00:59:31.080 --> 00:59:33.470
when I went to South Africa on my dad's tour

1284
00:59:33.470 --> 00:59:34.880
on his homecoming tour,

1285
00:59:34.880 --> 00:59:36.803
that's what caused the consternation with the church.

1286
00:59:36.803 --> 00:59:38.440
Yeah it come clean.

1287
00:59:38.440 --> 00:59:41.320
Had to come clean conscience is burning a hole in you

1288
00:59:41.320 --> 00:59:43.298
about what you did with.

1289
00:59:43.298 --> 00:59:44.370
Not well received.

1290
00:59:44.370 --> 00:59:45.847
Not well, I got kicked out.

1291
00:59:45.847 --> 00:59:47.180
(Rich laughs)

1292
00:59:47.180 --> 00:59:49.590
I got disfellowshipped from the church for.

1293
00:59:49.590 --> 00:59:51.390
That's the term disfellowshipped?

1294
00:59:51.390 --> 00:59:52.653
Yeah, for about a year.

1295
00:59:55.188 --> 00:59:57.200
When no one could talk to me.

1296
00:59:57.200 --> 00:59:58.120
That's rough, man.

1297
00:59:58.120 --> 00:59:59.810
I had to move out.
Is that your community?

1298
00:59:59.810 --> 01:00:01.270
Yeah, I had to move out of my parents' house

1299
01:00:01.270 --> 01:00:02.160
otherwise my stepfather

1300
01:00:02.160 --> 01:00:03.550
was gonna lose his position in the church.

1301
01:00:03.550 --> 01:00:05.640
You were 19?
19.

1302
01:00:05.640 --> 01:00:06.970
Yeah.
Wow.

1303
01:00:06.970 --> 01:00:08.204
That was a real one.

1304
01:00:08.204 --> 01:00:10.340
That was a real one, yeah.

1305
01:00:10.340 --> 01:00:12.300
But you moved to Carlsbad.

1306
01:00:12.300 --> 01:00:13.290
Moved to Carlsbad

1307
01:00:14.780 --> 01:00:16.600
and you know, we wake up in the morning

1308
01:00:16.600 --> 01:00:18.040
and you're in this place.

1309
01:00:18.040 --> 01:00:19.990
Like when we drove in that night, in the U-Haul

1310
01:00:19.990 --> 01:00:21.190
and then that next morning you wake up,

1311
01:00:21.190 --> 01:00:23.910
walk out the front door (inhales)

1312
01:00:23.910 --> 01:00:25.830
it smells different.

1313
01:00:25.830 --> 01:00:28.879
I never smelled this before and look up and like

1314
01:00:28.879 --> 01:00:31.720
it feel this air feels different.

1315
01:00:31.720 --> 01:00:34.640
Palm trees swaying, and I remember looking

1316
01:00:34.640 --> 01:00:38.630
panning right slowly and I recognize that we're on a hill

1317
01:00:38.630 --> 01:00:43.253
and about two miles away, a mile or two away is the ocean.

1318
01:00:44.140 --> 01:00:47.430
And I'm like, okay, I still don't.

1319
01:00:47.430 --> 01:00:49.590
There's no point of reference about surfing.

1320
01:00:49.590 --> 01:00:52.400
I'm just taking in where we're at.

1321
01:00:52.400 --> 01:00:54.530
And like, literally in that moment at moment

1322
01:00:54.530 --> 01:00:59.160
as we're unloading the U-Haul my stepfather and I

1323
01:00:59.160 --> 01:01:02.940
like a kid comes driving by in a purple Honda Revo scooter

1324
01:01:02.940 --> 01:01:07.139
with a full waterfall haircut, a tank top t-shirt,

1325
01:01:07.139 --> 01:01:12.139
board shorts, sandals and a book bag between his legs.

1326
01:01:13.090 --> 01:01:15.140
And I'm like, what is that?

1327
01:01:15.140 --> 01:01:16.700
That kind of alien?

1328
01:01:16.700 --> 01:01:19.100
What did I, like literally it was an alien

1329
01:01:19.100 --> 01:01:23.260
and in my mind is like he's going to school?

1330
01:01:23.260 --> 01:01:24.410
What?

1331
01:01:24.410 --> 01:01:28.070
In a couple of days later I go enroll in this school

1332
01:01:28.070 --> 01:01:30.000
and everyone's dressed like that.

1333
01:01:30.000 --> 01:01:33.400
And I'm like in literally like in like Timberland boots,

1334
01:01:33.400 --> 01:01:36.900
jeans, I've looked like I've come from another planet.

1335
01:01:36.900 --> 01:01:39.260
Everyone's speaking in this weird dialect.

1336
01:01:39.260 --> 01:01:42.550
And it said, you know, dude, and bro, and bra.

1337
01:01:42.550 --> 01:01:45.840
Right, you're like run D.M.C in their Spicoli.

1338
01:01:45.840 --> 01:01:48.140
Yeah, exactly.

1339
01:01:48.140 --> 01:01:53.140
And I met some cool kids when we went to church,

1340
01:01:55.350 --> 01:01:57.060
my mother was genius.

1341
01:01:57.060 --> 01:01:59.620
She was like, sure, we'll do your Spanish thing eventually

1342
01:01:59.620 --> 01:02:00.830
but we're starting in English.

1343
01:02:00.830 --> 01:02:03.480
She also had no intention to ever.

1344
01:02:03.480 --> 01:02:04.327
She's crafty.

1345
01:02:04.327 --> 01:02:06.520
Yes my mother was crafty, super crafty.

1346
01:02:06.520 --> 01:02:08.410
She is like, alright, sure.

1347
01:02:08.410 --> 01:02:11.370
But also like the compromised part was like

1348
01:02:11.370 --> 01:02:15.060
her alternative medicine crew

1349
01:02:15.060 --> 01:02:18.050
was like running heavy in Soquel.

1350
01:02:18.050 --> 01:02:19.097
Like she could be amongst her people

1351
01:02:19.097 --> 01:02:20.552
and not be aware of it.

1352
01:02:20.552 --> 01:02:21.385
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

1353
01:02:21.385 --> 01:02:22.240
North County, San Diego,

1354
01:02:22.240 --> 01:02:23.390
she's right where she wants to do that.

1355
01:02:23.390 --> 01:02:25.780
Right where she wants to be and she bought

1356
01:02:27.480 --> 01:02:30.660
a store, a sight unseen a business from a woman

1357
01:02:30.660 --> 01:02:34.470
who was in, worked with like one of the same herb companies

1358
01:02:34.470 --> 01:02:36.240
that she she dealt with.

1359
01:02:36.240 --> 01:02:38.323
And that was, we were supposed to be moving to a place

1360
01:02:38.323 --> 01:02:40.210
that had a storefront.

1361
01:02:40.210 --> 01:02:42.690
It turns out that the storefront was literally

1362
01:02:42.690 --> 01:02:46.480
the garage of this woman's house in a cul-de-sac

1363
01:02:46.480 --> 01:02:51.480
on a hill that had literally no traffic at all.

1364
01:02:52.990 --> 01:02:55.962
And there's no like Instagram to promote your business.

1365
01:02:55.962 --> 01:02:56.920
(Mabena laughs)

1366
01:02:56.920 --> 01:03:00.340
And it was like, oh, well this is different.

1367
01:03:00.340 --> 01:03:02.840
My mom, you know, realize like, oh,

1368
01:03:02.840 --> 01:03:04.460
I'm gonna have to build up a whole clientele

1369
01:03:04.460 --> 01:03:06.110
and it was a whole thing.

1370
01:03:06.110 --> 01:03:08.140
And shout out to my stepfather, you know,

1371
01:03:08.140 --> 01:03:13.107
for as much as we had a lot of shit that like, you know,

1372
01:03:15.880 --> 01:03:19.270
was a challenging relationship on many levels.

1373
01:03:19.270 --> 01:03:22.410
He didn't have work and he went out and became a day laborer

1374
01:03:22.410 --> 01:03:24.150
to like help make shit work,

1375
01:03:24.150 --> 01:03:28.150
like he was out working construction, you know,

1376
01:03:28.150 --> 01:03:30.220
on construction sites,

1377
01:03:30.220 --> 01:03:32.980
you know, making whatever $15 an hour.

1378
01:03:32.980 --> 01:03:35.280
And like, pretending like everything was good.

1379
01:03:36.280 --> 01:03:37.210
That's rough.

1380
01:03:37.210 --> 01:03:39.590
But it was rough before he was able to like establish

1381
01:03:39.590 --> 01:03:41.660
his thing and he started a pest control business

1382
01:03:41.660 --> 01:03:44.380
'cause that's what he did back East.

1383
01:03:44.380 --> 01:03:47.050
But these kids that I met at church went to my high school

1384
01:03:47.050 --> 01:03:49.400
and it turns out they were all surfers.

1385
01:03:49.400 --> 01:03:51.600
And a couple of them were like deaf

1386
01:03:51.600 --> 01:03:53.900
they weren't like super holy.

1387
01:03:53.900 --> 01:03:55.563
They were like, we're cool,

1388
01:03:56.410 --> 01:03:59.010
and this kid, Justin, he invited me to lunch

1389
01:03:59.010 --> 01:04:01.040
the next day at school and I was so stoked

1390
01:04:01.040 --> 01:04:03.170
to meet some kids that were cool.

1391
01:04:03.170 --> 01:04:06.540
And you could leave campus to go to lunch

1392
01:04:06.540 --> 01:04:08.410
which I had never experienced before the East.

1393
01:04:08.410 --> 01:04:10.490
Like we go to school, you're in prison

1394
01:04:10.490 --> 01:04:12.530
until it's time to get out.

1395
01:04:12.530 --> 01:04:15.690
And we went to this beach parking lot

1396
01:04:15.690 --> 01:04:18.450
after we'd gotten food at a place called Carl's Junior

1397
01:04:18.450 --> 01:04:20.390
that I didn't know existed.

1398
01:04:20.390 --> 01:04:22.330
And we're sitting in the car in the back of his,

1399
01:04:22.330 --> 01:04:25.260
I mean in the trunk of his Subaru hatchback with

1400
01:04:25.260 --> 01:04:27.340
and the car is packed with everybody,

1401
01:04:27.340 --> 01:04:28.720
like, you know, eating.

1402
01:04:28.720 --> 01:04:31.010
And they're looking out in the lineup

1403
01:04:31.010 --> 01:04:35.153
and I look up, I'm like, ah, no way, surfing.

1404
01:04:36.700 --> 01:04:38.930
And these guys are speaking in the language,

1405
01:04:38.930 --> 01:04:41.010
talking about what they're seeing waves and swell.

1406
01:04:41.010 --> 01:04:42.610
And I'm just sitting there quiet

1407
01:04:42.610 --> 01:04:46.010
like what I'm flashing back to a year ago in Bondai.

1408
01:04:46.010 --> 01:04:48.763
And I was like, hey, I've seen this before.

1409
01:04:49.700 --> 01:04:52.470
And they're like, whatever it did, I'm like, no

1410
01:04:52.470 --> 01:04:55.010
I was in a show here last year at Bondai beach

1411
01:04:55.010 --> 01:04:56.603
and they're like, now what?

1412
01:04:57.723 --> 01:05:00.140
(all laughs)

1413
01:05:01.330 --> 01:05:05.270
Pre-internet, like Bondai beach must've been like ass.

1414
01:05:05.270 --> 01:05:07.830
Yeah, I've seen it in magazines and movies.

1415
01:05:07.830 --> 01:05:11.425
And also mind you, there's like couple of guys.

1416
01:05:11.425 --> 01:05:13.530
You got a least likely guys you would ever imagined.

1417
01:05:13.530 --> 01:05:15.320
These two other black kids in the school.

1418
01:05:15.320 --> 01:05:19.640
One runs track and the other one plays football

1419
01:05:19.640 --> 01:05:21.110
and I've shown up.

1420
01:05:21.110 --> 01:05:22.790
And they're like, what are you talking about?

1421
01:05:22.790 --> 01:05:26.010
And then I'd tell him my story and like, Paul Simon,

1422
01:05:26.010 --> 01:05:28.470
my dad, they said like, who are you?

1423
01:05:28.470 --> 01:05:29.883
Like, I'm like, I wanna do that.

1424
01:05:29.883 --> 01:05:33.570
Like I saw this and I was like, if I ever get a chance,

1425
01:05:33.570 --> 01:05:35.380
I told Justin, like I want to try it

1426
01:05:35.380 --> 01:05:36.470
and he's like, all right,

1427
01:05:36.470 --> 01:05:37.820
be at my house on Saturday.

1428
01:05:38.980 --> 01:05:41.420
And that's how I got to experience surfing.

1429
01:05:41.420 --> 01:05:44.530
We went there and they had a wetsuit and board for me

1430
01:05:44.530 --> 01:05:48.120
and put the wetsuit on backwards, everyone laughed and

1431
01:05:48.120 --> 01:05:50.061
Right, whatever was it.

1432
01:05:50.061 --> 01:05:53.150
You ended up surfing like 170 days in a row, right?

1433
01:05:53.150 --> 01:05:54.330
Without missing a day?

1434
01:05:54.330 --> 01:05:55.710
Yeah, I surf.

1435
01:05:55.710 --> 01:05:57.880
So you are committed to figure this out.

1436
01:05:57.880 --> 01:06:00.600
The first time I stood up was like for five seconds.

1437
01:06:00.600 --> 01:06:02.330
And it was literally like,

1438
01:06:02.330 --> 01:06:06.210
I had gone to church thousands of times, you know,

1439
01:06:06.210 --> 01:06:09.320
prayed in groups, in large conventions

1440
01:06:09.320 --> 01:06:14.320
and in our, you know,

1441
01:06:15.040 --> 01:06:17.050
our three different religious meetings,

1442
01:06:17.050 --> 01:06:22.050
I never ever felt spirituality before.

1443
01:06:23.000 --> 01:06:28.000
I'd never felt a soul calling like I did at 16 and a half,

1444
01:06:28.280 --> 01:06:31.750
standing on a surf board for like five seconds.

1445
01:06:31.750 --> 01:06:34.100
Time stopped, everything changed.

1446
01:06:34.100 --> 01:06:37.070
It literally felt like whatever some spirit poured into me.

1447
01:06:37.070 --> 01:06:39.080
And when I came up after falling,

1448
01:06:39.080 --> 01:06:41.340
I screamed as loud as I could

1449
01:06:41.340 --> 01:06:44.350
and my whole life just pivoted

1450
01:06:44.350 --> 01:06:47.110
and it was like, okay, this is everything now.

1451
01:06:47.110 --> 01:06:48.417
And I'd never, it was.

1452
01:06:48.417 --> 01:06:50.060
You heard that clarity.

1453
01:06:50.060 --> 01:06:50.893
I heard that clarity,

1454
01:06:50.893 --> 01:06:53.290
it was the greatest spiritual awakening

1455
01:06:53.290 --> 01:06:56.060
that I could have ever asked for at the time.

1456
01:06:56.060 --> 01:06:59.130
Yeah, I mean, we could talk for two hours

1457
01:06:59.130 --> 01:07:00.750
about the spirituality of surfing,

1458
01:07:00.750 --> 01:07:02.250
but what's interesting is that

1459
01:07:03.510 --> 01:07:05.920
it was etched into your soul in a way

1460
01:07:05.920 --> 01:07:07.940
that almost makes it sound like

1461
01:07:07.940 --> 01:07:09.390
some kind of past life thing.

1462
01:07:09.390 --> 01:07:11.360
Like when you have that experience in Bondai

1463
01:07:11.360 --> 01:07:13.630
and then when you actually connected with it.

1464
01:07:13.630 --> 01:07:16.860
I mean, first of all, you have that experience of Bondai

1465
01:07:16.860 --> 01:07:19.130
and there's a knowingness about your path

1466
01:07:19.130 --> 01:07:22.180
on some conscious unconscious level.

1467
01:07:22.180 --> 01:07:25.570
Then the universe conspires to move you to, you know,

1468
01:07:25.570 --> 01:07:29.910
the epic surf spot in America, like completely,

1469
01:07:29.910 --> 01:07:33.230
I mean, nobody could have predicted a set of circumstances

1470
01:07:33.230 --> 01:07:34.940
that would bring that about,

1471
01:07:34.940 --> 01:07:38.960
and then the manner in which you just go all in on it

1472
01:07:38.960 --> 01:07:41.403
from the outset, like it's wild.

1473
01:07:42.810 --> 01:07:44.630
In retrospect, yeah.

1474
01:07:44.630 --> 01:07:45.520
You know?
Yeah.

1475
01:07:45.520 --> 01:07:48.022
I mean it just clicked

1476
01:07:48.022 --> 01:07:49.540
it was like, oh, this is me.

1477
01:07:49.540 --> 01:07:51.293
Everything associated with this.

1478
01:07:53.070 --> 01:07:55.200
I need to just soak.

1479
01:07:55.200 --> 01:07:59.460
I would go to the library at school

1480
01:07:59.460 --> 01:08:01.270
and read back issues of surfer magazine.

1481
01:08:01.270 --> 01:08:05.680
Like I had to learn the culture, the origins, everything.

1482
01:08:05.680 --> 01:08:09.380
And as you're coming of age

1483
01:08:09.380 --> 01:08:12.260
and you're dealing with all the challenges

1484
01:08:12.260 --> 01:08:15.340
of your teen anx and then the extra layers

1485
01:08:15.340 --> 01:08:17.570
of that were particular to my family

1486
01:08:17.570 --> 01:08:19.313
but every kid had a version of it.

1487
01:08:20.710 --> 01:08:23.780
Surfing became this mega oasis

1488
01:08:23.780 --> 01:08:25.560
for like none of that mattering

1489
01:08:25.560 --> 01:08:30.090
like the ultimate freedom of expression, of like

1490
01:08:30.090 --> 01:08:33.770
that was the one place that I could express myself wholly

1491
01:08:33.770 --> 01:08:38.120
that I couldn't do with my mom and stepdad

1492
01:08:38.120 --> 01:08:39.410
and I couldn't even do with my dad,

1493
01:08:39.410 --> 01:08:41.150
like surfing literally was that place

1494
01:08:41.150 --> 01:08:42.780
where I could just express

1495
01:08:42.780 --> 01:08:47.780
even though I had the tensions of a community

1496
01:08:48.030 --> 01:08:50.530
that thought they were the definition of the thing

1497
01:08:51.620 --> 01:08:56.620
and that they were allowing me to visit in their space

1498
01:08:57.030 --> 01:09:00.630
and people many times feeling uninhibited to tell me

1499
01:09:00.630 --> 01:09:01.800
that I shouldn't be out there

1500
01:09:01.800 --> 01:09:04.193
and using the N word, et cetera, et cetera.

1501
01:09:07.300 --> 01:09:11.050
None of that could actually get in the way of the thing.

1502
01:09:11.050 --> 01:09:13.450
Like when I stood up to do the thing,

1503
01:09:13.450 --> 01:09:15.640
there were no voices except mine.

1504
01:09:15.640 --> 01:09:16.473
Wow.

1505
01:09:17.430 --> 01:09:21.460
Yeah, meanwhile, your dad is modeling this example

1506
01:09:21.460 --> 01:09:25.800
of being fully expressed in his artistic voice.

1507
01:09:25.800 --> 01:09:28.530
You have your stepdad and your mom

1508
01:09:28.530 --> 01:09:32.050
kind of modeling their version of trying to connect with God

1509
01:09:32.050 --> 01:09:35.160
or, you know, being, you know spiritual examples

1510
01:09:35.160 --> 01:09:39.340
and you finding your own version of these two things

1511
01:09:39.340 --> 01:09:40.830
in a completely unique way

1512
01:09:40.830 --> 01:09:43.580
that probably confounded all of them.

1513
01:09:43.580 --> 01:09:46.020
Every (all laughs)

1514
01:09:46.020 --> 01:09:51.020
You're so accurate, bro, like they were flummoxed.

1515
01:09:54.250 --> 01:09:55.083
Right?

1516
01:09:55.083 --> 01:09:59.550
My mom, stepdad, dad, aunt, everyone was like

1517
01:09:59.550 --> 01:10:02.030
what is happening?

1518
01:10:02.030 --> 01:10:05.010
You know, I had a chance to get into a really good school

1519
01:10:05.010 --> 01:10:10.010
because my dad's sister is an incredible academic

1520
01:10:10.040 --> 01:10:12.460
and she had some relationships and she was a professor

1521
01:10:12.460 --> 01:10:14.940
like now we can get him in.

1522
01:10:14.940 --> 01:10:19.130
It was a historically black college on the East coast

1523
01:10:19.130 --> 01:10:21.233
and I just was like, I have no interest.

1524
01:10:22.590 --> 01:10:23.860
What?

1525
01:10:23.860 --> 01:10:27.920
Like, my dad, I'm working construction part-time,

1526
01:10:27.920 --> 01:10:29.720
I'm cleaning offices at night.

1527
01:10:29.720 --> 01:10:31.630
Like I'm doing whatever I can

1528
01:10:31.630 --> 01:10:33.440
to like support my shred habit

1529
01:10:33.440 --> 01:10:36.440
and my family is just like confused.

1530
01:10:36.440 --> 01:10:38.034
Working to live.
Working to live.

1531
01:10:38.034 --> 01:10:39.220
I think it's hard for people.

1532
01:10:39.220 --> 01:10:41.180
I mean, I'm like little bit older than you,

1533
01:10:41.180 --> 01:10:45.560
but it's hard for people to remember or to understand

1534
01:10:45.560 --> 01:10:49.820
how different their culture was then than it is now

1535
01:10:49.820 --> 01:10:52.640
in the sense that it wasn't a career path.

1536
01:10:52.640 --> 01:10:55.760
There was nothing that you could kind of aspire

1537
01:10:55.760 --> 01:10:58.970
to do professionally in this world whatsoever.

1538
01:10:58.970 --> 01:11:02.310
It was just a habit that you needed to feed, right?

1539
01:11:02.310 --> 01:11:06.220
It was a lifestyle and you found ways of supporting yourself

1540
01:11:06.220 --> 01:11:08.880
so you could do the thing and that's kind of where it ended.

1541
01:11:08.880 --> 01:11:10.007
That's exactly it.

1542
01:11:10.007 --> 01:11:13.060
And my whole, everything about me was like

1543
01:11:13.060 --> 01:11:16.937
how do I stay close to the thing so that I can grow in it?

1544
01:11:16.937 --> 01:11:20.290
And I have plenty of friends who left and went to school

1545
01:11:20.290 --> 01:11:22.190
and, you know, put the boards away

1546
01:11:22.190 --> 01:11:23.727
and then came home and got good jobs

1547
01:11:23.727 --> 01:11:26.830
and their parents had like had some little slush

1548
01:11:26.830 --> 01:11:29.150
waiting for them and they got married.

1549
01:11:29.150 --> 01:11:31.610
But there's also all those guys that are out in the lineup

1550
01:11:31.610 --> 01:11:34.550
that, you know, are doing that thing too, right?

1551
01:11:34.550 --> 01:11:37.920
Guys who are like 20 years older and had it for a long time.

1552
01:11:37.920 --> 01:11:40.320
And they, you know, life's hard on those dudes.

1553
01:11:40.320 --> 01:11:42.980
Yeah, life's hard on those dudes.

1554
01:11:42.980 --> 01:11:45.470
But you know, when I would show up at the

1555
01:11:45.470 --> 01:11:48.860
I really got into Dawn patrol and I'd get up, you know,

1556
01:11:48.860 --> 01:11:53.250
especially when I was in the janitorial business,

1557
01:11:53.250 --> 01:11:56.770
I would clean these offices and car dealerships all night

1558
01:11:56.770 --> 01:11:58.740
and then I would time it so that I got off

1559
01:11:58.740 --> 01:12:00.830
right when the sun was coming up

1560
01:12:00.830 --> 01:12:04.470
so I could be first one out, good conditions, empty lineups.

1561
01:12:04.470 --> 01:12:06.997
So I'm this young dude who's trying to play catch up,

1562
01:12:06.997 --> 01:12:09.710
like I need to surf as much as possible.

1563
01:12:09.710 --> 01:12:11.390
I didn't have the convenience to be like, oh,

1564
01:12:11.390 --> 01:12:12.380
it's blown out or whatever.

1565
01:12:12.380 --> 01:12:14.690
I'm just there out there every day and I'd be out.

1566
01:12:14.690 --> 01:12:19.240
So you get the old dogs who they're in this space of like,

1567
01:12:19.240 --> 01:12:20.138
well, I don't know how much longer

1568
01:12:20.138 --> 01:12:23.424
I'm gonna be getting to do this from here every day.

1569
01:12:23.424 --> 01:12:26.040
All right, and they've built their life around the thing

1570
01:12:26.040 --> 01:12:28.070
and had the ups and downs with it.

1571
01:12:28.070 --> 01:12:30.580
And I'd spend, you know, those mornings, like having coffee

1572
01:12:30.580 --> 01:12:32.950
with these old heads and seeing like

1573
01:12:32.950 --> 01:12:36.750
there they'd tell their stories and their love

1574
01:12:36.750 --> 01:12:39.260
for the thing in the midst of heartbreak, et cetera.

1575
01:12:39.260 --> 01:12:40.600
And I was just like

1576
01:12:40.600 --> 01:12:42.980
I was so enamored with those guys

1577
01:12:42.980 --> 01:12:45.720
'cause they just had no, there was no ego about the thing.

1578
01:12:45.720 --> 01:12:47.823
It's like, it's where they needed to be.

1579
01:12:49.980 --> 01:12:52.430
And I heard it there was a kinship there

1580
01:12:53.408 --> 01:12:56.308
in that surfing for me is like where I needed to be to be.

1581
01:12:59.180 --> 01:13:04.180
And so it was just as hard as it is when I look back on it,

1582
01:13:05.370 --> 01:13:07.260
like working two, three jobs at a time, et cetera,

1583
01:13:07.260 --> 01:13:08.093
that shit was hard.

1584
01:13:08.093 --> 01:13:10.430
But like the joy of like,

1585
01:13:10.430 --> 01:13:11.740
well, this is what's always waiting for

1586
01:13:11.740 --> 01:13:13.920
this is what I've created the space for.

1587
01:13:13.920 --> 01:13:16.440
Made it to that it didn't seem weird to me

1588
01:13:16.440 --> 01:13:19.880
that also the idea of like, what are you gonna do later?

1589
01:13:19.880 --> 01:13:21.040
It was like, there was no later.

1590
01:13:21.040 --> 01:13:22.990
Yeah.
There is right now.

1591
01:13:22.990 --> 01:13:24.530
And the relationship to doing anything professional

1592
01:13:24.530 --> 01:13:27.700
was only in the context of how it could serve the thing

1593
01:13:27.700 --> 01:13:29.590
that you wanted to do, which was to serve.

1594
01:13:29.590 --> 01:13:30.570
Yeah.
Right.

1595
01:13:30.570 --> 01:13:33.020
Which is the inverse of how most people

1596
01:13:33.020 --> 01:13:34.700
look at career paths.

1597
01:13:34.700 --> 01:13:38.361
Yeah, it was total, that's all it was.

1598
01:13:38.361 --> 01:13:43.080
And therefore I worked every job almost under the sun,

1599
01:13:43.080 --> 01:13:47.150
bank teller, et cetera, whatever that I could find,

1600
01:13:47.150 --> 01:13:50.950
a balance of two or three jobs that could support the space,

1601
01:13:50.950 --> 01:13:51.783
I was good.

1602
01:13:53.100 --> 01:13:57.440
Do you think that there was a seed of aspiration

1603
01:13:57.440 --> 01:13:58.410
underneath that though?

1604
01:13:58.410 --> 01:14:01.130
Like, did you think my life, you know,

1605
01:14:01.130 --> 01:14:03.320
is gonna lead me in a direction

1606
01:14:03.320 --> 01:14:06.660
that's a little bit larger than this or were you just good?

1607
01:14:06.660 --> 01:14:08.040
I was good.

1608
01:14:08.040 --> 01:14:12.180
I think that going to South Africa

1609
01:14:13.670 --> 01:14:17.870
to assistant role manage my dad's homecoming tour

1610
01:14:17.870 --> 01:14:20.930
was definitely the thing that made me come home

1611
01:14:20.930 --> 01:14:25.930
and start to really like understand my blackness

1612
01:14:27.470 --> 01:14:29.630
in this predominantly white world

1613
01:14:29.630 --> 01:14:33.213
and like starting to claim my space.

1614
01:14:35.150 --> 01:14:39.783
And then there was getting let go from that surf shop.

1615
01:14:45.140 --> 01:14:48.760
That was a really like traumatic experience for me

1616
01:14:48.760 --> 01:14:51.120
because I didn't know for a couple of months

1617
01:14:51.120 --> 01:14:53.120
that that's why I got let go.

1618
01:14:53.120 --> 01:14:53.953
They just told me that again.

1619
01:14:53.953 --> 01:14:56.650
Right, it's like some other kid who was working there that

1620
01:14:56.650 --> 01:14:58.269
His conscious was bothering him.

1621
01:14:58.269 --> 01:14:59.700
His conscious was bothering him

1622
01:14:59.700 --> 01:15:01.240
and I had gone to school with him

1623
01:15:01.240 --> 01:15:03.840
and he felt like he had to tell me so he could live.

1624
01:15:04.820 --> 01:15:07.640
And that was when I kind of was at a crossroads

1625
01:15:07.640 --> 01:15:11.110
where I was like, I don't know how,

1626
01:15:11.110 --> 01:15:15.790
but like, no one is taking this away from me.

1627
01:15:15.790 --> 01:15:18.650
I'm going to figure out a way

1628
01:15:18.650 --> 01:15:21.090
to remain as close to it as possible.

1629
01:15:21.090 --> 01:15:23.290
But it was just really burned in that

1630
01:15:23.290 --> 01:15:24.910
like, I'm not going anywhere.

1631
01:15:24.910 --> 01:15:27.550
Y'all, aren't gonna take this from me.

1632
01:15:27.550 --> 01:15:30.000
Well, I wanna go back to that South Africa trip

1633
01:15:32.010 --> 01:15:33.810
'cause we kind of glanced over that.

1634
01:15:33.810 --> 01:15:38.270
But that seems like it must've been pretty profound, right?

1635
01:15:38.270 --> 01:15:41.180
Like you're, this is basically at the end of apartheid

1636
01:15:41.180 --> 01:15:43.950
and Mandela has been released from prison

1637
01:15:43.950 --> 01:15:45.480
and your dad does his tour there

1638
01:15:45.480 --> 01:15:49.730
to essentially celebrate this changing of the guard.

1639
01:15:49.730 --> 01:15:51.570
Yeah.
And you get to meet Mandela,

1640
01:15:51.570 --> 01:15:53.020
right?
I do.

1641
01:15:53.020 --> 01:15:55.500
He's not president yet for a few more years

1642
01:15:55.500 --> 01:16:00.500
but everything about that trip was insane.

1643
01:16:00.570 --> 01:16:05.570
Like was new and in many ways startling.

1644
01:16:09.531 --> 01:16:14.100
I had experienced various levels of racism in America

1645
01:16:14.100 --> 01:16:18.190
but I had never been, I hadn't yet to be in a place

1646
01:16:18.190 --> 01:16:23.190
where like it was the systemic part was still present

1647
01:16:24.750 --> 01:16:26.733
and accepted as a way of life.

1648
01:16:27.630 --> 01:16:30.140
You know, the laws were just starting to come off the books

1649
01:16:30.140 --> 01:16:33.700
when I got there, and South Africa was changing

1650
01:16:33.700 --> 01:16:36.010
but it was still an apartheid system.

1651
01:16:36.010 --> 01:16:39.080
And you know, something as simple

1652
01:16:39.080 --> 01:16:41.890
as like greeting a white person in the airport

1653
01:16:41.890 --> 01:16:43.170
as you're waiting for your baggage

1654
01:16:43.170 --> 01:16:46.020
and them looking at you, like, why are you talking to me?

1655
01:16:46.950 --> 01:16:50.810
You know things that I took very much for granted at home

1656
01:16:50.810 --> 01:16:54.240
every single person that's working in the airport

1657
01:16:54.240 --> 01:16:57.040
or is wearing like a specific jumpsuit

1658
01:16:58.600 --> 01:17:01.790
that's either blue or yellow that identifies them

1659
01:17:01.790 --> 01:17:03.840
as a worker and they're all black

1660
01:17:03.840 --> 01:17:05.570
and everyone else's white.

1661
01:17:05.570 --> 01:17:07.900
Like, you know, all these weird things were like,

1662
01:17:07.900 --> 01:17:10.673
oh, this is interesting, you know?

1663
01:17:12.950 --> 01:17:17.640
But yeah, I got to meet my

1664
01:17:17.640 --> 01:17:18.680
on the second day that I was there,

1665
01:17:18.680 --> 01:17:20.430
I met my grandfather and my sister.

1666
01:17:21.720 --> 01:17:24.200
That I was named after my grandfather

1667
01:17:24.200 --> 01:17:28.660
and my dad hadn't seen his father until he had moved there.

1668
01:17:28.660 --> 01:17:31.813
He hadn't seen him for 25 years, 30 years.

1669
01:17:33.840 --> 01:17:35.120
So it was all new for me.

1670
01:17:35.120 --> 01:17:37.240
And like also like getting this now,

1671
01:17:37.240 --> 01:17:39.800
coming into this awareness of my South Africanness,

1672
01:17:39.800 --> 01:17:41.610
like, oh, this is a part of me

1673
01:17:41.610 --> 01:17:44.010
and this is all kind of starting to well up now.

1674
01:17:45.490 --> 01:17:49.160
But we were on tour somewhere

1675
01:17:49.160 --> 01:17:50.940
and my dad said we were going to a dinner.

1676
01:17:50.940 --> 01:17:52.940
He didn't tell me what was going on

1677
01:17:52.940 --> 01:17:56.210
but he said it was important I needed to dress well.

1678
01:17:56.210 --> 01:17:58.320
And we're there and there's a bunch

1679
01:17:58.320 --> 01:17:59.197
of different people there

1680
01:17:59.197 --> 01:18:00.780
and people are hanging milling about

1681
01:18:00.780 --> 01:18:05.050
and then a door opens and in walks,

1682
01:18:05.050 --> 01:18:07.910
you know, Nelson Mandela.

1683
01:18:07.910 --> 01:18:09.980
And on this, never forget like

1684
01:18:09.980 --> 01:18:14.980
the way he took over the room without saying anything

1685
01:18:17.450 --> 01:18:21.300
in the most humble power

1686
01:18:21.300 --> 01:18:24.073
that I've ever witnessed in my life.

1687
01:18:25.090 --> 01:18:27.890
And he said a cup of hellos to people

1688
01:18:27.890 --> 01:18:29.490
then he walked straight up to me

1689
01:18:30.807 --> 01:18:33.953
and he said, hello, you must be Selema.

1690
01:18:33.953 --> 01:18:37.584
I'm like, wait, what?

1691
01:18:37.584 --> 01:18:42.584
And he told me everything about my life up until that point

1692
01:18:42.850 --> 01:18:45.580
and how important it must be for me

1693
01:18:45.580 --> 01:18:48.260
that I'm getting to have this experience

1694
01:18:48.260 --> 01:18:52.180
and how it was going to make a difference

1695
01:18:52.180 --> 01:18:54.113
and how happy he was for me,

1696
01:18:55.490 --> 01:18:57.183
that this is what I was getting

1697
01:18:57.183 --> 01:19:00.090
and that this would be unforgettable.

1698
01:19:00.090 --> 01:19:01.290
Yeah.

1699
01:19:01.290 --> 01:19:02.440
It's heavy man.

1700
01:19:02.440 --> 01:19:04.010
And I realized that my dad

1701
01:19:04.010 --> 01:19:07.293
had been talking to Mandela about me.

1702
01:19:08.840 --> 01:19:12.640
Yeah, I mean, your dad's rolling deep with Mandela.

1703
01:19:12.640 --> 01:19:15.610
He's playing music in South Africa.

1704
01:19:15.610 --> 01:19:19.440
This whole country is shifting and you,

1705
01:19:19.440 --> 01:19:21.690
you know, kind of, you know, fly home.

1706
01:19:21.690 --> 01:19:23.190
You must've been thinking like I can't,

1707
01:19:23.190 --> 01:19:26.550
how could you possibly live up to

1708
01:19:26.550 --> 01:19:28.810
that type of legacy, right?

1709
01:19:28.810 --> 01:19:32.120
You're working part-time jobs, you're living to surf.

1710
01:19:32.120 --> 01:19:34.460
Are you starting to think like

1711
01:19:34.460 --> 01:19:36.770
I gotta start my shit out or I need to, you know,

1712
01:19:36.770 --> 01:19:40.670
I need to like figure out my version of what my dad's doing

1713
01:19:40.670 --> 01:19:44.493
or did you just release yourself from any of that pressure?

1714
01:19:45.970 --> 01:19:48.510
I had an experience in Durban

1715
01:19:48.510 --> 01:19:50.810
where the cops tried to arrest me for surfing.

1716
01:19:54.370 --> 01:19:56.770
One of the things that I knew.

1717
01:19:56.770 --> 01:19:58.440
They're famous for that there too, right?

1718
01:19:58.440 --> 01:19:59.960
There's a lot of black surfers in Durban.

1719
01:19:59.960 --> 01:20:00.970
Now.
Yeah.

1720
01:20:00.970 --> 01:20:03.020
But then there were not,

1721
01:20:03.020 --> 01:20:06.130
like the beach that I was at a few months before

1722
01:20:06.130 --> 01:20:07.480
it was a whites only beach.

1723
01:20:08.520 --> 01:20:12.330
And from economic standpoint, socioeconomic standpoint,

1724
01:20:12.330 --> 01:20:13.950
no black person would have, like,

1725
01:20:13.950 --> 01:20:16.580
within that Durban region would have had access to surfing

1726
01:20:16.580 --> 01:20:18.420
to even challenge the thing.

1727
01:20:18.420 --> 01:20:19.760
So here along comes me

1728
01:20:19.760 --> 01:20:21.880
who was just like dying to get to the coast

1729
01:20:21.880 --> 01:20:24.360
because I know what North beach is,

1730
01:20:24.360 --> 01:20:25.400
I've seen the magazines,

1731
01:20:25.400 --> 01:20:28.092
I'm familiar with the Gunston 500 contests

1732
01:20:28.092 --> 01:20:30.360
I'm like, I'm gonna get to surf there.

1733
01:20:30.360 --> 01:20:35.060
And I was so angry I had to experienced that by that point.

1734
01:20:35.060 --> 01:20:37.750
So much crazy shit had taken place, almost died

1735
01:20:39.230 --> 01:20:43.020
because we got attacked and chased in our tour bus

1736
01:20:43.020 --> 01:20:47.570
by a bunch of like violently racist off the common people

1737
01:20:47.570 --> 01:20:49.030
who thought there were too many black people

1738
01:20:49.030 --> 01:20:51.400
inside of our story that we had stopped in

1739
01:20:51.400 --> 01:20:55.430
to get some provisions as we were driving to the coast

1740
01:20:55.430 --> 01:20:57.030
and we were in a big tour bus, et cetera.

1741
01:20:57.030 --> 01:20:58.170
And these guys came and chased us

1742
01:20:58.170 --> 01:21:02.390
in a pickup truck with guns and bats and knives.

1743
01:21:02.390 --> 01:21:05.000
And it was only because of an army convoy

1744
01:21:05.000 --> 01:21:06.430
that happened to be making a cross

1745
01:21:06.430 --> 01:21:08.960
after they'd thrown their trucks in front of our bus

1746
01:21:08.960 --> 01:21:10.820
to pull us over and attack

1747
01:21:10.820 --> 01:21:15.320
that we didn't end up in a much worse situation.

1748
01:21:15.320 --> 01:21:16.760
And so when we got to Durban,

1749
01:21:16.760 --> 01:21:20.750
I was like get me to the ocean.

1750
01:21:20.750 --> 01:21:23.750
I had money in my pocket, walked into a surf shop.

1751
01:21:23.750 --> 01:21:27.740
I walked into the Spider Murphy surf shop

1752
01:21:27.740 --> 01:21:30.350
and I bought a six, three and they were just,

1753
01:21:30.350 --> 01:21:31.987
people were just like

1754
01:21:31.987 --> 01:21:33.820
and I told them where I was from, et cetera,

1755
01:21:33.820 --> 01:21:35.220
and like, okay and I had the cash

1756
01:21:35.220 --> 01:21:39.370
but I still didn't realize how strange it might be

1757
01:21:39.370 --> 01:21:41.050
my dad warn me.

1758
01:21:41.050 --> 01:21:43.957
And you know, the first time when I went to go paddle out

1759
01:21:43.957 --> 01:21:48.340
and I came down the elevator and the hotel

1760
01:21:48.340 --> 01:21:50.210
was maybe like three blocks from the beach.

1761
01:21:50.210 --> 01:21:55.210
Elevator door opened and everyone in the lobby stopped

1762
01:21:55.320 --> 01:21:57.117
and looked at me.

1763
01:21:57.117 --> 01:21:59.020
And it was like, there was, you know,

1764
01:21:59.020 --> 01:22:01.070
people bustling business, et cetera.

1765
01:22:01.070 --> 01:22:05.350
It was the guests of the hotel and the black workers.

1766
01:22:05.350 --> 01:22:08.470
And I'm just standing there and it's like a standoff.

1767
01:22:08.470 --> 01:22:10.320
And I remember being like, do I walk out?

1768
01:22:10.320 --> 01:22:11.153
Do I not walk out?

1769
01:22:11.153 --> 01:22:11.986
Do I walk out do I not?

1770
01:22:11.986 --> 01:22:15.080
It was that much of a moment.

1771
01:22:15.080 --> 01:22:17.100
And there was a really nice woman

1772
01:22:18.146 --> 01:22:21.090
who was working behind the desk and she smiled at me

1773
01:22:21.090 --> 01:22:22.330
and I walk straight towards her.

1774
01:22:22.330 --> 01:22:24.390
And as I walked, no one, like

1775
01:22:24.390 --> 01:22:25.400
got back to what they were doing.

1776
01:22:25.400 --> 01:22:29.230
They just all kind of stopped and looked and turned.

1777
01:22:29.230 --> 01:22:31.110
And she said, I see you going surfing today.

1778
01:22:31.110 --> 01:22:33.580
I said, yeah, I went to hand her my keys.

1779
01:22:33.580 --> 01:22:35.280
She said, have a nice day.

1780
01:22:35.280 --> 01:22:36.847
I was like, okay.

1781
01:22:36.847 --> 01:22:39.627
And I handed her my key and I have my board

1782
01:22:39.627 --> 01:22:41.737
