02:07:56.350 --> 02:07:57.520
doing shit for free

2799
02:07:57.520 --> 02:08:00.110
and learning from people who like knew how to do it.

2800
02:08:00.110 --> 02:08:02.393
Right, but at some point then you gotta,

2801
02:08:04.070 --> 02:08:06.010
you know, know your value, right?

2802
02:08:06.010 --> 02:08:09.110
And like, when you start transitioning out of,

2803
02:08:09.110 --> 02:08:13.140
hey, I'm no longer your free guy, I gotta pay the rent.

2804
02:08:13.140 --> 02:08:14.550
Yeah.
Right, becomes tricky

2805
02:08:14.550 --> 02:08:17.966
especially if you're a people pleaser or you feel like

2806
02:08:17.966 --> 02:08:18.799
you know, you're the guy

2807
02:08:18.799 --> 02:08:19.830
who when you get the business card,

2808
02:08:19.830 --> 02:08:21.260
you're not gonna call the guy back.

2809
02:08:21.260 --> 02:08:24.820
Rich it is way too insightful.

2810
02:08:24.820 --> 02:08:26.350
Well, it's cause I relate.

2811
02:08:26.350 --> 02:08:29.380
I'm like, you know, I'm the same guy in a lot of ways.

2812
02:08:29.380 --> 02:08:31.090
Yeah, it was that for me.

2813
02:08:31.090 --> 02:08:32.970
Even after, you know what?

2814
02:08:32.970 --> 02:08:35.193
When I got my break at the E.S.P.N,

2815
02:08:36.160 --> 02:08:39.150
I had gone on to start a brand, a clothing company

2816
02:08:39.150 --> 02:08:42.030
with my with a bunch of friends called Alphanumeric.

2817
02:08:42.030 --> 02:08:43.820
That was a very impactful

2818
02:08:44.690 --> 02:08:47.263
sort of birth of the streetwear movement.

2819
02:08:49.040 --> 02:08:52.090
We were the first black and brown owned

2820
02:08:52.090 --> 02:08:55.850
founded clothing brand in the space.

2821
02:08:55.850 --> 02:08:59.430
We had a partnership of a bigger governmental brand

2822
02:08:59.430 --> 02:09:00.380
that like funded us,

2823
02:09:00.380 --> 02:09:02.847
but it was all run by a bunch of young black and brown kids

2824
02:09:02.847 --> 02:09:07.550
and we made waves like really impacted the industry heavily.

2825
02:09:07.550 --> 02:09:12.550
And it was such a cool thing after like kind of, you know,

2826
02:09:12.760 --> 02:09:14.680
working hard to find my way I jumped around

2827
02:09:14.680 --> 02:09:16.730
at different companies and people would be like,

2828
02:09:16.730 --> 02:09:18.120
what does he do?

2829
02:09:18.120 --> 02:09:19.680
Like, I know he can sell et cetera

2830
02:09:19.680 --> 02:09:21.730
but like what are you, what you can, and he's happy.

2831
02:09:21.730 --> 02:09:23.830
He's the guy that you're happy to be around,

2832
02:09:23.830 --> 02:09:25.820
but like what can he do, you know?

2833
02:09:25.820 --> 02:09:28.910
And so starting that brand was like, here's what I can do.

2834
02:09:28.910 --> 02:09:31.470
And we're making a difference.

2835
02:09:31.470 --> 02:09:35.400
And I was at an event with my team of athletes

2836
02:09:35.400 --> 02:09:37.690
I was the team manager marketing guy

2837
02:09:37.690 --> 02:09:41.370
and we're in Colorado and a guy walked up to me

2838
02:09:42.290 --> 02:09:45.120
after the first day of competition of the events,

2839
02:09:45.120 --> 02:09:46.720
triple crown of snowboarding, he said,

2840
02:09:46.720 --> 02:09:49.830
are you Sal Masekela?

2841
02:09:49.830 --> 02:09:51.107
And I said, yeah.

2842
02:09:51.107 --> 02:09:53.323
And he said, my name is Phil Orlins.

2843
02:09:54.420 --> 02:09:56.870
I'm the producer of snowboarding from the E.S.P.N X Games

2844
02:09:56.870 --> 02:09:58.210
